  'beeDesc.cleaner': 'For their first few days workers clean cells, clearing out the cocoons and debris left after emergence so the queen will lay in them again.',
  'beeDesc.nurse': 'Nurses eat pollen and secrete royal jelly from glands in their heads to feed the larvae. A larva is fed over a thousand times a day.',
  'beeDesc.house': 'House bees secrete wax to build cells and fan to evaporate water from nectar; when the honey is ripe and the cell full they seal it with wax. While flowers are open some wait near the entrance, take nectar mouth to mouth from returning foragers and store it; fewer do so when the hive is full of honey. When nurses are short some house bees go back to feeding larvae; when foragers are short they start foraging early.',
  'beeDesc.guard': 'Guards stand at the entrance and check the smell of every bee, keeping out bees from other colonies and hornets.',
  'beeDesc.forager': "Workers spend the last two or three weeks of life foraging. Once a forager knows a patch it keeps going back; it hands nectar to house bees at the entrance and packs pollen into cells itself. After a good trip it dances on the comb: a waggle dance for far patches, where the angle of the run from straight up is the angle between the patch and the sun and a longer waggle means farther; a round dance for near ones. Other foragers follow the dance, read off the place and fly out to search. A forager that waits too long to unload does the tremble dance, calling more house bees to take nectar, and may give up on the patch; that is how a hive full of honey brings in less.",
  'beeDesc.drone': "Drones don't work and have no sting; they eat honey and wait to fly out to mate.",
  'beeDesc.queen': 'A colony has a single queen, much longer than the workers. In summer she lays hundreds of eggs a day while workers feed and groom her.',

